#!/bin/bash
# Real bug found via repeated real full install + reboot tests on
# actual UEFI hardware, reproduced in a VM (2026-09-23): even after
# Release 14's fix (setting @root as the filesystem's default btrfs
# subvolume, confirmed correct at the raw block level -- a plain
# `mount /dev/sdX3 /mnt` with no -o subvol= option correctly showed
# @root's real content, including /boot/loader/entries), GRUB's own
# generated grub.cfg (which uses `blscfg` to dynamically read
# /boot/loader/entries/*.conf at real boot time) still produced a menu
# with no OS entry at all, only the auto-generated "UEFI Firmware
# Settings" fallback. GRUB's own built-in btrfs filesystem driver is a
# separate, from-scratch reimplementation (not the Linux kernel's own
# btrfs driver/VFS mount logic) and evidently does not resolve the
# on-disk default-subvolume pointer the same way a real `mount` does
# -- confirmed by the entry still not appearing even after the
# default-subvolume fix that fixed the same raw partition when
# inspected via a normal kernel mount.
#
# Rather than continue chasing exactly which internal blscfg/GRUB-
# btrfs-driver behavior is responsible, this bypasses blscfg's dynamic
# discovery entirely: it reads the same, already-correct BLS entry
# kernel-install wrote (title/linux/initrd/options, already using the
# real /@root/-prefixed paths GRUB needs for this subvolume layout --
# see kernel-install's own real output for the "why" of that prefix)
# and writes it out as a real, static `menuentry` block into
# custom.cfg, which this profile's grub.cfg (via its own stock
# /etc/grub.d/41_custom template section) already sources
# unconditionally from the same directory as grub.cfg itself. A static
# menuentry needs no dynamic BLS discovery at all, so whatever GRUB's
# own blscfg/btrfs-subvolume resolution gap actually is becomes
# irrelevant.
# Replaced 2026-09-26 (ticket #54): the static custom.cfg entry was
# pinned to the install-time kernel, and GRUB's full config sat on the
# EFI partition where kernel updates never regenerate it. parchaos-boot's
# parchaos-grub-setup (installed in the target) now writes the real
# config to /boot/grub2/grub.cfg and a small EFI stub that loads it.
set -e
exec /usr/libexec/parchaos-grub-setup
